The tuples() method groups items into tuples but drops items to make them fit. For example:
use itertools::Itertools;
let mut v : Vec<(i32, i32)> = Vec::new();
for x in (1..6).tuples::<(_,_)>() {
    v.push(x);
}
assert_eq!(v, vec![(1,2), (3,4)]);
Note that the range 1..6 contains the element 5, but the grouped vector does not include it in a tuple. Hence, the latest snapshot (the newly added one) is not included in the set of tuples if the number of snapshots is odd. The way the following code uses tuples() causes incorrect TWAP calculation:
let duration_times_funding_rate = snapshots.iter().tuples::<(_, _)>().fold(
    Lifted::zero(),
    |acc, (previous, current)| {
        acc + Lifted::from(current.at - previous.at)
        * Lifted::from(current.funding_rate)
    },
);
let result = duration_times_funding_rate
/ Lifted::from(now - snapshots.front().map(|x| x.at).unwrap_or_default());
Worse, the calculation does not calculate a weighted average. It uses only half of the snapshot price points (those bound to the current variable in the fold function). It only considers the time difference between grouped pairs, ignoring the other time differences. If we have five snapshots, s1 through s5, the calculation of the new funding rate becomes:
(s2.at − s1.at)(s2.funding_rate) + (s4.at − s3.at)(s4.funding_rate)
s5.at − s1.at
The intended calculation is:
P5 i=2(si.at − si−1.at)(si.funding_rate)
s5.at − s1.at

